## /factory sync

Read-only on {{ cfg.connections.tickets.provider }}; the only file it changes is `.factory/backlog.md`.
Never pass `--push`: creating issues from backlog items is the user's, from the studio's Sync with
Jira button or `factory sync --push` in a terminal (the guard blocks it here).
{% if cfg.connections.tickets.mode == "mcp" %}
1. **Query** with the Atlassian MCP (`searchJiraIssuesUsingJql`):
   `{{ cfg.connections.tickets.jql }}`, fields `summary`, `issuetype`, `status`, `parent`, `labels`.
2. **Dedupe.** Skip a key that appears on any backlog line (any state) or in any
   `.factory/jobs/*/job.json` `ticket` field.
3. **Resolve the base.** No epic: `{{ cfg.repo.default_base }}`. Epic: `git fetch {{ cfg.connections.git.remote }} --prune`, then
   `git branch -r --list '{{ cfg.connections.git.remote }}/{{ cfg.connections.tickets.epic_branch_glob.replace("{epic}", "<EPIC-KEY>") }}'`. Exactly one match: that branch
   without `{{ cfg.connections.git.remote }}/`. {% if cfg.connections.tickets.no_epic_branch == "fallback" %}No match: `{{ cfg.repo.default_base }}`.{% else %}No match: blocked, `epic <EPIC-KEY> has no branch on {{ cfg.connections.git.remote }}`.{% endif %}
   Several: blocked, `epic <EPIC-KEY> has several branches: <list>`.
4. **Write** new items under `## Queue`, keeping existing lines (an existing `- [!]` line whose
   epic branch now resolves becomes `- [ ]` with its base):
   `- [ ] KEY · base: <branch> · <summary>` or `- [!] KEY · <reason> · <summary>`.
5. **Report** one line per ticket: added, unblocked, skipped or blocked with the reason.
{% else %}
`python3 .factory/factory.py sync` and relay its report.
{% endif %}
{% endif -%}
## /factory next

{% if tickets and cfg.connections.tickets.mode == "mcp" -%}
1. Read `.factory/backlog.md` and take the first `- [ ]` line (skip `- [!]`).
2. If it has a ticket key, re-check it with `getJiraIssue`: if it left the To Do status category,
   replace the line with `- [x] <rest> → <status>`; if it lost the `{{ cfg.connections.tickets.label }}` label,
   `- [x] <rest> → unlabelled`; then try the next line.
3. Claim it with one Edit that replaces its exact line with `- [x] <rest> → claimed`. If the Edit
   fails because the line changed, another session claimed it: re-read and try the next.
4. Run **/factory <KEY or text> --base <base from the line>**. Once you have the job id, replace
   `claimed` with it.
{%- else -%}
`python3 .factory/factory.py next --json` claims the first `- [ ]` backlog line and creates its
job. Then run the step loop with that job id.
{%- endif %}

## /factory ui [stop | <port>]

Run `.factory/ui.sh` (or `.factory/ui.sh stop`, or `.factory/ui.sh <port>`) and relay its output.
It serves `.factory/` on 127.0.0.1 (default port {{ cfg.ui.port }}) and opens the dashboard. For
editing the factory itself (agents, skills, the loop, connections), the user runs `factory studio`.

## Reporting

One short line per transition, for example
`004-{{ ticket_example }}-project-rename: round 2, security CHANGES, code PASS, ui skipped → resuming builders`.
When the job stops, say whether it is ready (with the commands), delivered (with the link),
waiting for an approval, or needs a human, and why.
